{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYWsxm78qpipBaENxhU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"OpenAI's updated Model Spec, published October 29, comes with a qualification: its five values are not intended to capture the full range of human values the models should respect and help people pursue. They organize the document and guide how models handle some of the most important trade-offs, and OpenAI describes them as bringing together commitments already spread across the Spec.\n\nBeyond faithfulness to human intent, the four others have specific meanings. Minimizing harm means respecting firm safety boundaries and accounting for the foreseeable consequences of the model's actions. Honesty and transparency mean being truthful about what the model knows, does and is uncertain about, so people can evaluate its reasoning and work. Facilitating human agency means strengthening people's ability to understand, choose and act for themselves. Care means attending to the circumstances of those affected, following relevant social and professional norms, and doing work people can rely on. OpenAI ties these to its mission of ensuring that artificial general intelligence benefits all of humanity, which it says requires considering longer-term interests and effects on others as well as someone's immediate intentions.\n\nNaming the values gives readers something concrete to dispute. OpenAI asks which parts of the Spec people find compelling, where they disagree with the balance it strikes, and what it overlooks or leaves unclear. My inference is that disagreement can now be more precise, such as whether a particular default gives agency or harm minimization the right weight, though the article does not identify which trade-offs are hardest.\n\nThe article also leaves the governance unchanged. OpenAI writes the Spec and sets its boundaries, and it says it refines the document through research, deployment experience, public feedback and discussions with users and outside experts. It does not describe how feedback is weighed against those other inputs, so how much outside criticism changes the next version is still unknown."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1241,"cache_read_input_tokens":7952,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1241},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a078e45d-a647-4fa3-9837-d88646382f77","uuid":"ab0c0594-df95-42e4-b76d-bcac9f1d9d57","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:24:44.065Z","request_id":"req_011CgdYWsjcu4eVT8KCcRHbM"}
